2 TITL History of Sweden - The Early Vasa era (1523–1611) - Wikipedia
1 NOTE King of Sweden.
2 CONT Stamfar for de senere Svenske konger.
1 NOTE Uppfostrades vid Sten Sture d.y. hov. Gisslan hos Kritian II i Danmark, hölls
2 CONT i förvar på Kalö på Jylland. Rymde 1519 till Lübeck. Till Sverige 1520.
2 CONT Valdes till kung 1523. Krönt 1526.
1 NOTE He was the founder of the Swedish royal house of Vasa and creator of an 
2 CONT independent Sweden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustav was educated at the University of Uppsala. As a young man he 
2 CONT entered the army, and from 1518 to 1519 he fought against Christian II, 
2 CONT king of Denmark, who had invaded Sweden to regain the throne that the 
2 CONT Danes had lost in 1501. Gustav was taken hostage but managed to escape. He 
2 CONT was still a fugitive when he heard of Christian's massacre of his father 
2 CONT and other nationalist Swedish nobles in Stockholm in 1520. With an army of 
2 CONT peasants, Gustav led a successful revolt (1521-23) and drove the Danes out 
2 CONT of Sweden. His victory resulted in the dissolution of the Union of Kalmar, 
2 CONT which in 1397 had placed Sweden and Norway under the Danish crown. Gustav 
2 CONT was administrator of the kingdom from 1521 to 1523, when the Riksdag, or 
2 CONT national assembly, elected him king. He strengthened his political hold 
2 CONT and restored order by introducing the Reformation into Sweden. The Roman 
2 CONT Catholic church had supported Christian II, and Gustav used this as an 
2 CONT excuse for stripping the church of political power and confiscating its 
2 CONT property. In 1527 he made Swedish bishops subject to royal power, and in 
2 CONT 1529 he proclaimed Lutheranism the state religion. He joined with Norway 
2 CONT in 1537 to defeat the German city of Lübeck, making Sweden economically 
2 CONT independent of the Hanseatic League. Gustav created a strongly united 
2 CONT state by introducing a sound financial administration, strengthening 
2 CONT manufacturing, trade, and agricultural interests, and increasing the 
2 CONT military forces. In 1544 he made the Swedish crown hereditary through his 
2 CONT line, the house of Vasa.
