2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Royal mistress
2 CONT
2 CONT The position seems to have been quite official, as she was given nice clothes and appeared with him openly at court, and was given her own apartment and servants. Thus, she could be regarded as the first official royal mistress in
2 CONC  Sweden, although only Hedvig Taube otherwise is considered an official royal mistress in Sweden. When her daughter Sigrid was born in 1566, she was treated as though she was a legitimate princess. Before this, the king had a whol
2 CONC e harem of mistresses, such as Agda Persdotter and Doredi Valentinsdotter, but when Karin entered his life, he dismissed them all. His treatment of her caused much astonishment. She also received education and learned to read and 
2 CONC write.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin Månsdotter was never portrayed (a portrait long believed to be of her has been discovered to be of her sister-in-law), but she is described as a very beautiful girl with long blond hair and innocent eyes, and her personality
2 CONC  seems to have been calm, humble and natural. The king was mentally unstable, and she seems to have been the only one who could comfort him and calm him down, which made her appreciated by his relatives, who considered her good fo
2 CONC r him. She had no personal enemies at court, but she was not respected, and their marriage in 1568 was considered a scandal and may have contributed to his dethronement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin Månsdotter, Eric XIV and Jöran Persson, in Georg von Rosen's painting of 1871
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin Månsdotter, Eric XIV and Jöran Persson, in Georg von Rosen's painting of 1871
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unknown wether Karin Månsdotter had any political influence, but a popular image in Swedish history was of her serving as a counterweight to the king's advisor Jöran Persson; a painting by Georg von Rosen three hundred years
2 CONC  later (1871) illustrates this, showing the king on the floor, confused by his inner demons, with Karin Månsdotter at one side holding his hand, looking like an innocent angel giving him strength to resist the demands of Jöran Per
2 CONC sson, standing on the other side of him, trying to get him to sign a document.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her contemporarys early used her to give Eric appeals on their behalf, especially the nobles at court did this, and it seems as she did her best to do so, which is illustrated in the Sture Murders in Uppsala 1567, which could perh
2 CONC aps describe the form of her influence on Eric. In May 1567, Eric is considered to have suffered from some sort of mental collapse. He had gattered several men from the noble family Sture, among them count Svante sture, and impris
2 CONC oned them. Countess Märta Sture, sister of the fomrer queen Margaret Leijonhufvud, sent an appeal to Karin Månsdotter and asked that the prisoners be protected. Karin sent for her and asured her that no none will hurt the prisoner
2 CONC s. Later the same morning, the king visited Svante Sture in prison, fell on his knees before him and begged for his friendship. Later the same day, however, the Sture prisoners was executed all the same.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Queen
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric XIV married Karin morganatically in 1567, and officially in 1568, when she was ennobled and crowned queen under the name Katarina Magnusdotter (a fancified version of her name). The coronation was celebrated with great festiv
2 CONC ities in Stockholm to confirm the new queen's legitimacy. The wedding was unique; never before had the children of the couple been present at a royal wedding; both the infant son and daughter of Eric and Karin were present to conf
2 CONC irm their official status. Karin's relatives were dressed in clothes made for them by the royal tailor. During the coronation, the Lord Chancellor, who was carrying the crown, fainted and dropped the crown to the floor. Immediatel
2 CONC y afterward, Eric's brothers rebelled and he and his family were imprisoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said, that during the dethronement, Karin fell on her knees before Queen Dowager Katarina Stenbock, crying out; "Forgive him!", as a reminder of the insanity of the King, of which the wedding was considered a sign, and one o
2 CONC f the reasons for the coup, and then to the king's brother, the future King John III of Sweden, crying out the same thing; "Forgive him!", but she was completely ignored. This touching scene, portrayed in an old film about her, wa
2 CONC s most likely a myth - among other things, the Queen Dowager had left the castle at that time - but it illustrates the probably truthful image of her and her personality.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Karin and her children were separated from her husband in 1573 to prevent the birth of any more legitimate offspring. King Eric described it in his diary as: " My wife has been taken from me by use of violence." Karin and he
2 CONC r children were taken to the Castle of Turku (Åbo) in Finland where she remained under house arrest until the death of her husband four years later. In 1575, her son was taken from her and sent to Poland to be placed under the car
2 CONC e of the Jesuits, but she was allowed to keep her daughter. In 1577, she received the news of her husband's death, but she was treated with kindness and given a manor in Kangasala, Finland, where she lived the rest of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Widowhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin Månsdotter's monumental grave in the Cathedral of Turku
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin Månsdotter's monumental grave in the Cathedral of Turku
2 CONT
2 CONT She returned to the Swedish court on two occasions; in 1577, newly widowed, she travelled to Stockholm to ask for economic support (which she was given) and in 1582, she met Queen Catherine Jagiellon and Queen Dowager Katarina Ste
2 CONC nbock in what was called the "Three Queens Visit".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1587, her daughter Sigrid was appointed lady-in-waiting to the new king's daughter, Princess Anna of Finland, who followed her brother King Sigismund to Warsaw where he had been elected king. Karin accompanied her on her journe
2 CONC y. In Warsaw, she met her son Gustaf again, twelve years after he was taken from her. He was now a Catholic, he had forgotten her and they could not speak to each other because he had forgotten the Swedish language, and Karin knew
2 CONC  no other language; she could identify him only by the help of his birthmarks. She saw him once again in Estonia in 1595, and unlike the previous meeting, this has been confirmed. He was poor and was working as a mercenary. She tr
2 CONC ied to help him financially, and for the rest of her life, she tried to get permission for him to return to Sweden, but she never saw him again. Her daughter Sigrid, on the other hand, married two Swedish noblemen and often spent 
2 CONC time with her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin became respected and liked in Finland; during the great rebellion Cudgel War in 1596-1597, the rebels refrained from plundering her estate. She is buried in the Cathedral of Turku.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although three of the queens in Sweden during the same century were not of royal blood, (but noble), Karin Månsdotter was the only Swedish queen before modern times to be a commoner, and her fate has been regarded as quite unique 
2 CONC and romantic.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin Månsdotter had the following children with the king;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Sigrid of Sweden (1566–1633) (born before the marriage), lady-in-waiting, wife of two noblemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Gustav of Sweden (1568–1607) (born before the marriage), mercenary.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henrik (1570–1574)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Arnold (1572–1573)
2 CONT
