1 OCCU Queen of Sweden 1585-1592
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Gunilla Bielke=
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunilla_Bielke '''Gunilla Bielke'''] was Queen of Sweden as the second spouse [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_III_of_Sweden '''King John III''']. Queen Gunilla is acknowledged to have acted as th
2 CONC e political adviser to John III and to have influenced his religious policies in favor of Protestantism.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early life==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gunilla Bielke was''' the daughter of the cousin of John III, the former governor of Östergötland Johan Axelsson Bielke, and Margareta Axelsdotter Posse. Orphaned early, she was raised at the royal court since she was ten years
2 CONC  old as a playmate of the King's daughter, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Vasa_of_Sweden '''Anna''']. In 1582, she was made maid of honour to the Queen, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Jagiellon '''Catherine Jagiello
2 CONC n'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''About half a year''' after the death of his first queen in 1583, John III chose her to be his next consort, after first having considered [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigrid_Brahe '''Sigrid Brahe''']. Bielke is described by h
2 CONC er contemporaries as a beautiful blonde, though the portraits preserved by her are considered to be too stylistic to give a true image of her appearance. It was acknowledged that the reason to the marriage was that the king was at
2 CONC tracted to her beauty, and this was also the reason John III himself stated for his reason to marry a member of his own nobility rather than a foreign princess. Asked why he did not marry a member of a princely house, he openly st
2 CONC ated that he wished to have a beautiful consort and that portraits of foreign princess was not to be trusted. Therefore, it would be wiser: "''To marry a person in this country, of which whom you have already seen''."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Similar to the mother''' and step-mother of John III, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Leijonhufvud '''Margaret Leijonhufvud'''] and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Stenbock '''Catherine Stenbock'''], Gunilla wa
2 CONC s engaged when the King decided to marry her. She was engaged to the noble Per Jonsson Liljesparre, and initially refused the king's proposal on her own initiative. According to tradition, the King was so enraged by her refusal th
2 CONC at he slapped her in the face with his gloves. Her family, however, forced her to agree, retract her refusal, and consent to the marriage. Her engagement was broken, and her wedding to the king was arranged.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The marriage was''' controversial and regarded by the king's family, who opposed it, to be a misalliance. His sisters were enraged. They considered it a misalliance because of the difference in rank, despite the fact that their
2 CONC  own mother was also a member of the nobility, a fact he pointed out to them without effect.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Queen==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The wedding ceremony''' took place in Västerås the 21 February 1585, followed by the coronation of Gunilla the following day. Because of the controversy around the marriage, the monarch was eager to make the occasion as impress
2 CONC ing as possible, and arranged much celebrations around the event, such as a tournament, where queen Gunilla officiated by handing out the price to the winners. The King's siblings, however, all refused to attend the wedding. The o
2 CONC nly exception was his sister Sophia, who was resident in Sweden and dependent upon his allowance for her income. For this, he punished his sisters economically and entered into a long going conflict with his brother Charles. Gunil
2 CONC la was given a very large allowance and is regarded as one of the richest of the queens of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Queen Gunilla had a''' significant influence on John III. She is credited with having influenced his policy regarding religion in favor of Protestantism, similar to the way his first spouse, Catherine Jagiellon, had influenced 
2 CONC him in favor of Catholicism. After his marriage to her, his work to introduce a counter reformation in Sweden effectively ceased, and she is known to have acted in favor of persecuted anti-liturgical priests on several occasions.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gunilla accompanied John''' III to the council in Reval in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estonia_under_Swedish_rule Swedish Estonia] in 1589, where the monarch came into conflict with the Councillors of the noble parliamentary
2 CONC  estate. She acted as a mediator between the monarch and the deposed Councillors during the conflict. Eventually, in 1590, the King released the political prisoners, members of the parliament, on her request, among them her former
2 CONC  guardian Hogenskild Bielke.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The King himself openly''' admitted to having changed his opinion and his decisions in various subjects "''for the sake of our mistress the dear Queen's wishes''." During the last years of the reign of John III, a significantly
2 CONC  large amount of documents were issued in the name of the queen, displaying her large influence upon state affairs. She successfully accepted to make recommendations for supplicants, and even the siblings of her spouse did ask her
2 CONC  to speak to the king on their behalf.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The political influence of''' queen Gunilla was not uncontroversial. Rather, it has been regarded in a more negative light then the equally large influence of her predecessor, because Gunilla was a member of the nobility, and s
2 CONC he was accused of using her influence to benefit her relatives. Her brother-in-law Duke Charles, the future [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_IX_of_Sweden '''King Charles IX'''], has portrayed her in a very bad light in this 
2 CONC regard and accused her of using her illegitimate influence over the king to promote her family with a rime in his chronicle:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Med drottning Gunnel vele vi tale<br/>
2 CONT  hon kan oss mycket hugsvale,<br/>
2 CONT  hos konungen kan hon fodra bäst.<br/>
2 CONT  ty hon är vår vän och släkten bäst<br/>
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Of Queen Gunnel we now shall say
2 CONT  <br/>
2 CONT  that she can benefit us all so well,<br/>
2 CONT   with the king she will always be successful<br/>
2 CONT   because she is our friend but most of all she is her family's)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1590, John III left''' instructions for queen Gunilla to serve as regent, should his son with her succeed to the throne while being still a minor. The son of Gunilla and John III, however, never succeeded to the throne, bein
2 CONC g put aside first by his elder half brother and then by his uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''When the son of''' Charles XI, king [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustavus_Adolphus_of_Sweden '''Gustav II Adolf'''] of Sweden, wished to marry another member of the nobility, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebba_Brahe '''Ebba 
2 CONC Brahe'''], in the 1610s, his mother [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_of_Holstein-Gottorp '''Queen Christina'''] opposed the match with a reference to the marriage between John III and Gunilla Bielke as a bad example, and e
2 CONC ventually prevented it.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Queen dowager==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King John III died in''' 1592. According to the chronicle of ''Aegidius Girs'', queen Gunilla stayed by the bedside of the dying John III, holding his hand, while holding the hand of their son with the other. He was succeeded b
2 CONC y his son in his first marriage, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigismund_III_Vasa '''King Sigismund'''], the step-son off Gunilla, who was in Poland at the time of his father's death. Queen dowager Gunilla refused to leave the Ro
2 CONC yal Castle in Stockholm before the arrival of Sigismund to Sweden, eager to had the support of Sigismund in protecting the large inheritance she had been given in the will of her late spouse against her former brother-in-law Duke 
2 CONC Charles. Duke Charles, who had been updated by constant reports of the king's health during his illness, claimed that Gunilla had concealed the death of the late monarch for days while stealing from the Royal Castle's belongings.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Upon the arrival of''' Sigismund and his consort [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_of_Austria,_Queen_of_Poland '''Anna of Austria'''] to Sweden in 1593, Gunilla demanded that an inventory be made to clear her of Charles's acc
2 CONC usations and presented the will of the late king to prove that her demands were legal. Duke Charles protested that the property left to her in the will of the late king was so preposterously large that the will should be declared 
2 CONC illegal even if it was indeed the will of the late king. Queen Anna also accused Gunilla of having deprived the royal castle of most of its belongings, making it uncomfortable and bare, and the two queens also clashed on religious
2 CONC  reasons.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On 18 May 1594''', however, Sigismund confirmed the inheritance of queen dowager Gunilla. She retired to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Br%C3%A5borg_Castle Bråborg Castle] in the Duchy of her son, Östergötland, where she reside
2 CONC d until her death. She was given an unusually generous allowance and lands in the will of her spouse, as well as sole and complete custody of her son, and thereby of his Duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gunilla died of the''' fever and was interred in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uppsala_Cathedral Uppsala Cathedral].
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children==
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John,_Duke_of_%C3%96sterg%C3%B6tland John, Duke of Östergötland].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Fiction===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gunilla Bielke is the''' main character in the 1983 novel Stackars lilla drottning - Historisk roman om Gunilla Bielke, Johan III:s unga gemål (Poor little queen - A historical novel about Gunilla Bielke, the young consort of J
2 CONC ohn III) by Anna Sparre.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
