2 TITL Gustaf Gustafsson af Vasaborg – Wikipedia
1 NOTE Because of his brilliant leadership of the Protestant forces in the Thirty 
2 CONT Years' War, became known as the Lion of the North. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he succeeded to the throne in 1611, Sweden was at war with Denmark, 
2 CONT Russia, and Poland. Gustav concluded a peace with Denmark in 1613, but 
2 CONT Sweden regained its southern provinces only after agreeing to pay heavy 
2 CONT financial indemnities. He waged a successful war against Russia (1613-17), 
2 CONT acquiring lands that completely cut off Russian access to the Baltic Sea. 
2 CONT From 1621 to 1629, Gustav waged a war against his cousin, Sigismund III, 
2 CONT king of Poland, who maintained a claim to the Swedish throne. Poland was 
2 CONT forced to cede lands and cities along the southern and eastern Baltic 
2 CONT coasts in 1629, and Gustav's right to the Swedish throne was ensured. A 
2 CONT religious interest in the Protestant cause and a belief that conquest of 
2 CONT northern Germany by the Holy Roman Empire would be militarily and 
2 CONT economically dangerous to Sweden impelled Gustav to enter the Thirty 
2 CONT Years' War. After securing an alliance with France, he landed his army on 
2 CONT the coast of Pomerania and succeeded in driving the imperial forces back 
2 CONT from the Baltic. His victory at the Battle of Breitenfeld in 1631 gave the 
2 CONT Protestants the military advantage, and they went on to occupy Bavaria and 
2 CONT Bohemia. Gustav turned his army north in 1632. At the Battle of Lützen in 
2 CONT Saxony, he defeated the imperial forces, but was himself fatally wounded. 
2 CONT Gustav was noted not only as a great general but as a capable 
2 CONT administrator. With the help of his chancellor, Count Axel Oxenstierna, 
2 CONT who managed internal affairs and diplomacy while the king was involved in 
2 CONT military campaigns, Gustav developed a sound and centralized system of 
2 CONT government and did much to develop the natural mineral resources of his 
2 CONT country. He was succeeded by his daughter, Christina.
